7.2 Code reading CONFIRMED. In analysis_from (identical function in both
carriers): Pw_exact is the exact Gaussian window probability; cant =
Vdet/(Vdet + Edet^2) for Edet > 0, else 1 (valid one-sided Cantelli bound on
P(det <= 0)); sq = sqrt(max(D2, 0)) with D2 the (grad-conditioned) second
moment of det H; and the shipped bound is literally
    wp_exact = pgrad * sq * min(cant, Pw_exact)
The probability factor enters LINEARLY.
7.3 Mathematics. Given grad f = 0 and the 9 pins, put U = |det H| and
V = 1{det H < 0, f in [b - ell, b]}. Cauchy-Schwarz gives
E[U V] <= sqrt(E[U^2]) * sqrt(P(det < 0, f in W))
       <= sqrt(D2) * sqrt(min(cant, Pw_exact)).
Since 0 <= min(cant, Pw_exact) <= 1, the implemented quantity is LESS THAN
OR EQUAL to the valid CS bound, with shortfall factor
1/sqrt(min(cant, Pw_exact)) -- of order 1/sqrt(Pw), i.e. roughly 1e2-1e4 per
cell at these window widths (ell = r^3/6). No alternative route in the
certificate certifies the linear-P form: D2 is grad-conditioned only (not
window-conditioned), and f-H dependence is nonzero (cvec != 0 -- the truth
engines exist precisely because of it). A window-conditioned second moment
WOULD make a linear-P bound valid (Section 9 route iii); that is not what
is implemented. VERDICT: DEF-WP-CS-01 CONFIRMED. The implemented wp is an
independence-style heuristic that happens to majorize the truth on the
computed grids; it is not a certified upper bound.
